WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.465
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.190
PRESENTER: I'd like
now to introduce to you

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.830
Professor Stephen Hawking.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:20.040
In your handouts, you'll find
a very elaborate introduction.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.120
And you will find very
interesting elements in it.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.660
As a distinguished
educator, researcher,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.150
and public communicator,
Stephen Hawking

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:34.560
has had a transforming effect
in his area, and indeed,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.510
in all of science.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.300
We are deeply appreciative of
his commitment to be with us

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.060
and to share his views of
the importance of applying

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.550
technology to disabled persons.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.834
Professor Stephen Hawking.

00:00:46.834 --> 00:00:50.313
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.556
HAWKING: Can you hear me?

00:01:51.556 --> 00:01:52.139
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.700
HAWKING: Technology
has transformed

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.185
the outlook for the disabled.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:27.510
People like me can now
move around independently,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.120
and they can communicate.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.820
The fact that you
are listening to me

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.035
now shows what
technology can do even

00:02:38.035 --> 00:02:41.180
if it does give me a
Scandinavian or American

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.288
accent.

00:02:42.288 --> 00:02:46.112
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.920
The main problem
of communicating

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.690
for people like
me who can't speak

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.940
is what is called
the baud rate, that

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:05.740
is the rate at which
information can be conveyed.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:12.890
Normal speed is between
120 and 180 words a minute.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:18.800
By contrast, a reasonable typist
can do 40 to 60 words a minute.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.250
Thus, if one were to equip
normal people with keyboards,

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.540
they could communicate
at between half

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.565
and a quarter of
the speech rate.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.030
However, many people
who can't speak

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:40.570
also have other
disabilities, like me,

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:41.935
and can't use a keyboard.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.630
Instead, they have to use
one or more switches operated

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:50.900
by a head or hand movement.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.340
This is where the information
flow problem really

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.493
confronts one.

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:07.556
One might be able to operate
a switch two or three times

00:04:07.556 --> 00:04:08.055
a second.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.950
This would mean that one
could send information

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:19.480
at a rate of two or
three bits a second.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.370
One bit of information is the
answer to a yes/no question.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:29.700
By contrast, if you
take an average work

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.490
to be about five
characters, and you

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:36.600
assume that any character can
follow any other character,

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:41.700
normal speech has an information
flow rate of between 50 and 75

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.805
bits a second.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:51.650
However, the real information
flow in human speech

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.850
is much less than this.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:57.560
In the case of
political speeches,

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.054
it has practically zero.

00:05:00.054 --> 00:05:04.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.830
This is because spelling out
a sentence letter by letter

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.290
is inefficient.

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:22.550
Most sequences of letters
don't make recognizable words,

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:26.750
let alone meaningful sentences.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:30.525
So there's a lot of redundancy
in specifying every letter.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.650
It is much more
efficient to pick

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:41.095
words, or even whole phrases,
from a list of likely ones.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:48.060
In the occasional cases that the
words or phrases don't appear

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.720
on the list, one can always
resort to spelling them

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.560
out letter by letter.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.930
To translate the
presses of a switch

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:10.340
into letters, words, or
sentences requires computers.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:14.220
The development of
microprocessors in the last 15

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.040
years has meant that virtually
unlimited computing power is

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.840
available at a reasonable cost.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.710
What one needs is
sufficient software

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:32.490
to translate the input from
the switch into sentences.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.630
I can manage about
15 words a minute

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.165
with the equalizer
program that I use.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.170
That is not too bad, since an
information flow rate of three

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:53.970
bits a second corresponds
to 25 to 30 words a minute.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.890
But obviously, there's
scope for improvement.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:10.380
Having decoded the signals
from some input device,

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:13.740
such as a switch, one
then has to broadcast it

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:18.100
in a form people can understand.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:22.025
This could be visually, with
text or diagrams on a screen.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:28.860
But if you want to
communicate like others,

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.030
which is what people in
my position would like,

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:33.110
you need to speak.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.690
Computerized speech
synthesizers have improved

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.000
a great deal in recent years.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.990
And this is important.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:52.170
Not only does one
want to be understood,

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.480
but also one doesn't
want to sound

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.615
like Mickey Mouse or a Dalek.

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:06.310
This voice of mine may
be a bit tinny and Irish,

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:07.735
but it is almost human.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.880
There are other ways
in which technology

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.075
can assist the disabled.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:29.780
For instance, I have infrared
and radio environment control

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:33.330
with which I can open
doors, turn on lights,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.010
and operate the television.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.805
I also have a mobile phone that
I can use to call for help.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.770
If one is unfortunate
enough to be disabled,

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:52.880
this is the age
in which to be so.

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.690
I probably wouldn't
have survived,

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:01.610
and I certainly
wouldn't have been

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:04.360
able to write a
best-selling book,

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:08.280
I had been born any earlier.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.305
The disabled have a lot
to thank technology for.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.800
That is all I have to
say for the moment.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:25.340
Thank you for listening.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:29.290
[APPLAUSE]

